<footer class="site-footer">
  <div class="footer-inner">
    <div class="footer-brand">
      <%= link_to root_path, class: "brand" do %><%= render "shared/logo" %><span>Spike or Stay</span><% end %>
      <p>Tells Shopify merchants whether a product's surge in public interest is likely to last. A v0 built on Wikipedia pageviews.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="footer-col">
      <h4>Product</h4>
      <%= link_to "Dashboard", dashboard_path %>
      <%= link_to "Fidget spinner replay", dashboard_path(as_of: "2017-06-12") %>
      <%= link_to "Air fryer replay", dashboard_path(as_of: "2017-12-31") %>
    </div>
    <div class="footer-col">
      <h4>Method</h4>
      <%= link_to "How it works", methodology_path %>
      <%= link_to "Backtest results", methodology_path(anchor: "results") %>
      <%= link_to "Limitations", methodology_path(anchor: "limitations") %>
    </div>
    <div class="footer-col">
      <h4>Help</h4>
      <%= link_to "FAQ", root_path(anchor: "faq") %>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="footer-legal">
    <%# Computed from backtest/results/step_d_predictions.csv (BacktestStats), analysis (a). %>
    <p>
      Model backtested on n = <%= TrendModel.instance.trained_on["n"] %> past trends: leave-one-out accuracy <%= BacktestStats.percent(:a, :logreg) %> (95% CI <%= BacktestStats.interval(:a, :logreg) %>)
      vs <%= BacktestStats.percent(:a, :naive) %> for "still rising in week 6". The difference is within noise at this sample size. Labels describe public
      interest (Wikipedia pageviews), not sales. Confidence is the model's probability, not a guarantee.
    </p>
  </div>
</footer>
